University of Georgia">SUB2543303</SUBMITTER_ID></IDENTIFIERS></SUBMISSION><Organization type="institute"><Name>University of Georgia</Name><Address postal_code="30602"><Department>Crop and Soil Science</Department><Institution>University of Georgia</Institution><Street>3111 Carlton, Miller Plant Sci Bldg</Street><City>Athens</City><Sub>GA</Sub><Country>United States of America</Country></Address><Contact email="jared.wilmoth@gmail.com"><Address postal_code="30602"><Department>Crop and Soil Science</Department><Institution>University of Georgia</Institution><Street>3111 Carlton, Miller Plant Sci Bldg</Street><City>Athens</City><Sub>GA</Sub><Country>United States of America</Country></Address><Name><First>Jared</First><Last>Wilmoth</Last><Middle>Lee</Middle></Name></Contact></Organization><STUDY center_name="BioProject" alias="PRJNA381321" accession="SRP103306"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRP103306</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="BioProject" label="primary">PRJNA381321</EXTERNAL_ID></IDENTIFIERS><DESCRIPTOR><STUDY_TITLE>Oxygenation rate during Fe redox-cycling affects humid tropical forest soil metatranscriptome</STUDY_TITLE><STUDY_TYPE existing_study_type="Other"/><STUDY_ABSTRACT>Soils were collected from the Bisley Watershed, Luquillo Critical Zone Observatory, Puerto Rico. These humid tropical forest soils are hypothesized to impact global carbon and greenhouse gas cycling through Fe biogeochemical reactions during redox-oscillating conditions. This study used a laboratory incubation of these soils to investigate the effects of oxygenation rate on iron mineral transformations and microbial iron(III)-reducer gene expression (RNAseq) during alternating anoxic:oxic conditions.</STUDY_ABSTRACT></DESCRIPTOR></STUDY><SAMPLE alias="1_3_R1_fastq" accession="SRS2110135"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRS2110135</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="BioSample">SAMN06674021</EXTERNAL_ID><SUBMITTER_ID namespace="pda|jaredleewilmoth" label="Sample name">1_3_R1_fastq</SUBMITTER_ID></I